WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.533
Exclusive new

00:00:00.533 --> 00:00:01.134
reporting on a

00:00:01.134 --> 00:00:03.036
previously unreported episode in the Mar

00:00:03.036 --> 00:00:04.704
a Lago documents case.

00:00:04.704 --> 00:00:06.306
It comes as attorneys for the former

00:00:06.306 --> 00:00:06.740
president

00:00:06.740 --> 00:00:07.173
met today

00:00:07.173 --> 00:00:08.074
with Justice Department

00:00:08.074 --> 00:00:10.310
officials and signs increasingly indicate

00:00:10.677 --> 00:00:12.045
that special counsel Jackson's

00:00:12.045 --> 00:00:13.847
probe could be in its final stages.

00:00:13.847 --> 00:00:14.447
Seen as Kaitlan

00:00:14.447 --> 00:00:15.849
Collins is here with more.

00:00:15.849 --> 00:00:17.150
So walk us through this exclusive

00:00:17.150 --> 00:00:18.518
reporting about Mar a Lago. Okay.

00:00:18.518 --> 00:00:20.153
It's a bit of a bizarre story,

00:00:20.153 --> 00:00:21.321
so bear with me.

00:00:21.321 --> 00:00:23.623
But it was in last October.

00:00:23.623 --> 00:00:25.625
We are told that this maintenance worker

00:00:25.625 --> 00:00:27.660
at Mar a Lago drained the pool.

00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:29.262
And when they drained the pool,

00:00:29.262 --> 00:00:31.865
it caused a flood that flooded this room

00:00:32.165 --> 00:00:33.199
that we are told

00:00:33.199 --> 00:00:34.734
has the computer servers

00:00:34.734 --> 00:00:36.436
which store the surveillance footage

00:00:36.436 --> 00:00:37.103
on them.

00:00:37.170 --> 00:00:37.837
That's important

00:00:37.837 --> 00:00:39.005
because we know prosecutors

00:00:39.005 --> 00:00:40.040
have been looking

00:00:40.040 --> 00:00:41.541
at the surveillance footage from Mar

00:00:41.541 --> 00:00:41.941
a Lago.

00:00:41.941 --> 00:00:43.510
They've subpoenaed it several times.

00:00:43.510 --> 00:00:44.310
They've requested that

00:00:44.310 --> 00:00:46.446
they preserve records several times.

00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:48.681
What we don't know is whether or not

00:00:48.681 --> 00:00:50.483
this was an intentional flooding

00:00:50.483 --> 00:00:51.851
or if it was genuinely,

00:00:51.851 --> 00:00:53.353
genuinely a mistake.

00:00:53.353 --> 00:00:54.687
But the maintenance worker

00:00:54.687 --> 00:00:55.588
who was the one

00:00:55.588 --> 00:00:57.590
who drained the pool in October,

00:00:57.590 --> 00:00:59.559
which is the busy season at Mar a Lago,

00:00:59.559 --> 00:01:00.760
also I should note,

00:01:00.760 --> 00:01:01.261
is someone

00:01:01.261 --> 00:01:01.995
who is caught

00:01:01.995 --> 00:01:04.230
the eye of the prosecutors here.

00:01:04.230 --> 00:01:05.698
They've seized his phone.

00:01:05.698 --> 00:01:06.966
They've talked to him.

00:01:06.966 --> 00:01:08.101
He's someone who was seen

00:01:08.101 --> 00:01:09.269
on surveillance footage

00:01:09.269 --> 00:01:11.604
that they have moving boxes at Mar

00:01:11.604 --> 00:01:11.905
a Lago.

00:01:11.905 --> 00:01:13.406
We don't know exactly which boxes,

00:01:13.406 --> 00:01:14.474
but he was moving boxes

00:01:14.474 --> 00:01:15.909
with another Trump aide.

00:01:15.909 --> 00:01:17.110
But when it comes to

00:01:17.110 --> 00:01:18.111
whether or not

00:01:18.111 --> 00:01:19.312
the role that he played here,

00:01:19.312 --> 00:01:20.747
they have a lot of questions about this

00:01:20.747 --> 00:01:22.382
because we are told

00:01:22.382 --> 00:01:23.950
that someone has testified

00:01:23.950 --> 00:01:25.018
to these prosecutors

00:01:25.018 --> 00:01:26.519
that nothing was damaged

00:01:26.519 --> 00:01:28.321
when this flood happened in this room

00:01:28.321 --> 00:01:29.689
where these servers restored,

00:01:29.689 --> 00:01:31.357
that kept the surveillance footage.

00:01:31.357 --> 00:01:32.792
But they view it as suspicious

00:01:32.792 --> 00:01:34.060
and they've been asking witnesses

00:01:34.060 --> 00:01:36.196
about it and raising questions about it.

00:01:36.196 --> 00:01:36.763
And, of course,

00:01:36.763 --> 00:01:38.031
they're investigating whether or not

00:01:38.031 --> 00:01:39.632
there was any obstruction

00:01:39.632 --> 00:01:40.767
and their investigation.

00:01:40.767 --> 00:01:42.802
So I think it's just an odd instance

00:01:42.869 --> 00:01:44.404
that raises a lot of questions. Will?

00:01:44.404 --> 00:01:46.739
Yeah, it certainly has.

00:01:46.739 --> 00:01:49.309
All the video been given handed over

00:01:49.309 --> 00:01:50.910
that was on the servers. Do we know?

00:01:50.910 --> 00:01:51.377
We're told

00:01:51.377 --> 00:01:52.812
that we certainly know a lot of

00:01:52.812 --> 00:01:53.880
it has been given over,

00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:55.348
but they have been raising questions

00:01:55.348 --> 00:01:56.282
about what we're told.

00:01:56.282 --> 00:01:57.450
We learned so much of this, of

00:01:57.450 --> 00:01:59.052
what investigators are asking people.

00:01:59.052 --> 00:02:00.053
They've interviewed

00:02:00.053 --> 00:02:01.955
basically everyone, I'm told,

00:02:01.955 --> 00:02:02.755
but they have asked

00:02:02.755 --> 00:02:04.791
what gaps in surveillance footage.

00:02:04.791 --> 00:02:06.526
We know that they've had questions

00:02:06.526 --> 00:02:08.862
for the security guys who were running

00:02:09.429 --> 00:02:10.196
and in charge

00:02:10.196 --> 00:02:11.197
of the surveillance footage.

00:02:11.197 --> 00:02:12.298
So there have been questions

00:02:12.298 --> 00:02:13.366
among that nature

00:02:13.366 --> 00:02:15.668
of what exactly was turned over.

00:02:15.668 --> 00:02:16.903
Trump Trump attorneys have said

00:02:16.903 --> 00:02:18.171
that everything has been turned over.

00:02:18.171 --> 00:02:19.439
This was technically up to the Trump

00:02:19.439 --> 00:02:21.641
Organization that got these subpoenas.

00:02:21.641 --> 00:02:22.575
But I still think it raises

00:02:22.575 --> 00:02:24.210
a lot of questions just given, of course,

00:02:24.210 --> 00:02:25.111
what we've seen play out.

00:02:25.111 --> 00:02:25.979
What do we know about the meeting

00:02:25.979 --> 00:02:26.846
between Trump lawyers

00:02:26.846 --> 00:02:28.348
and the Justice Department today? Yeah.

00:02:28.348 --> 00:02:28.982
So that was today.

00:02:28.982 --> 00:02:29.716
So Trump attorneys

00:02:29.716 --> 00:02:31.351
going into the Justice Department,

00:02:31.351 --> 00:02:32.385
I'm told Jack Smith,

00:02:32.385 --> 00:02:33.253
the special counsel

00:02:33.253 --> 00:02:34.220
who is investigating

00:02:34.220 --> 00:02:35.288
the classified documents

00:02:35.288 --> 00:02:37.357
and January six was in that meeting.

00:02:37.624 --> 00:02:38.691
They requested a meeting

00:02:38.691 --> 00:02:39.559
from the Trump side

00:02:39.559 --> 00:02:41.027
with Attorney General Merrick Garland.

00:02:41.027 --> 00:02:42.028
They wrote him a letter.

00:02:42.028 --> 00:02:42.662
They allege

00:02:42.662 --> 00:02:43.663
that there was some

00:02:43.663 --> 00:02:45.532
prosecutorial misconduct here.

00:02:45.532 --> 00:02:46.733
They didn't really say what it was

00:02:46.733 --> 00:02:48.168
pretty broad strokes

00:02:48.168 --> 00:02:49.035
we know obviously,

00:02:49.035 --> 00:02:50.103
though, typically

00:02:50.103 --> 00:02:51.437
before someone is indicted,

00:02:51.437 --> 00:02:52.172
their attorneys would like

00:02:52.172 --> 00:02:52.972
to meet with them.

00:02:52.972 --> 00:02:54.073
That's not what we're told

00:02:54.073 --> 00:02:56.209
was the content of this meeting today.

00:02:56.209 --> 00:02:57.877
I think it was raising questions

00:02:57.877 --> 00:02:59.112
about the special counsel's

00:02:59.112 --> 00:02:59.879
investigation.

00:02:59.879 --> 00:03:01.014
They've certainly had some

00:03:01.014 --> 00:03:02.081
some broad complaints.

00:03:02.081 --> 00:03:03.583
Nothing really specific

00:03:03.583 --> 00:03:05.018
about how this has been handled.

00:03:05.018 --> 00:03:05.752
But they were inside

00:03:05.752 --> 00:03:06.452
the Justice Department

00:03:06.452 --> 00:03:08.254
for about 90 minutes today.

00:03:08.254 --> 00:03:09.355
I think what's most telling is

00:03:09.355 --> 00:03:10.857
what Trump was saying after

00:03:10.857 --> 00:03:12.325
because he was posting,

00:03:12.325 --> 00:03:13.793
why should he be charged

00:03:13.793 --> 00:03:14.694
with no other presidents

00:03:14.694 --> 00:03:15.628
have been charged.

00:03:15.628 --> 00:03:16.663
We don't know that they

00:03:16.663 --> 00:03:17.297
they told him

00:03:17.297 --> 00:03:18.631
he was going to be charged for anything,

00:03:18.631 --> 00:03:21.100
but he was certainly referencing.

00:03:21.634 --> 00:03:22.268
I know you're

00:03:22.268 --> 00:03:23.136
going to be anchoring at nine.

00:03:23.136 --> 00:03:24.137
You got the former U.N.

00:03:24.137 --> 00:03:25.405
Ambassador John Bolton

00:03:25.405 --> 00:03:26.673
on the program, worked also obviously

00:03:26.673 --> 00:03:27.540
in the Trump administration.

00:03:27.540 --> 00:03:28.775
We'll watch for that right now

00:03:28.775 --> 00:03:29.609
to get a better sense

00:03:29.609 --> 00:03:31.311
of what goes on in these meetings

00:03:31.311 --> 00:03:31.844
that attorneys

00:03:31.844 --> 00:03:32.812
have with prosecutors,

00:03:32.812 --> 00:03:33.613
as well as what goes into

00:03:33.613 --> 00:03:35.415
the ultimate decision on indictments.

00:03:35.415 --> 00:03:35.682
We're joined

00:03:35.682 --> 00:03:36.549
by CNN senior

00:03:36.549 --> 00:03:37.717
law enforcement analyst and former

00:03:37.717 --> 00:03:39.552
FBI deputy director Andrew McCabe.

00:03:39.552 --> 00:03:40.954
So what likely happens in a meeting

00:03:40.954 --> 00:03:42.855
like this between Trump's attorneys and

00:03:42.855 --> 00:03:43.823
DOJ officials?

00:03:45.558 --> 00:03:45.992
Sure.

00:03:45.992 --> 00:03:47.093
So, Anderson, it's

00:03:47.093 --> 00:03:49.095
good to also know that

00:03:49.095 --> 00:03:51.030
avoiding getting your client indicted

00:03:51.030 --> 00:03:52.432
is every defense attorney's

00:03:52.432 --> 00:03:54.234
first and biggest goal.

00:03:54.234 --> 00:03:56.236
And so the last ditch effort

00:03:56.236 --> 00:03:58.137
to try to avoid an indictment

00:03:58.137 --> 00:04:00.273
is usually a meeting along these lines.

00:04:00.273 --> 00:04:02.642
The defense attorneys request to come in

00:04:02.642 --> 00:04:04.711
and basically explain to DOJ

00:04:04.711 --> 00:04:06.980
why an indictment would be a bad idea.

00:04:07.647 --> 00:04:09.415
It's a bit of a Hail Mary pass

00:04:09.415 --> 00:04:09.916
like you would see

00:04:09.916 --> 00:04:10.950
at the end of a football game,

00:04:10.950 --> 00:04:13.086
one last ditch effort to avoid disaster.

00:04:13.386 --> 00:04:15.388
What happens is the attorneys come in

00:04:15.388 --> 00:04:16.322
and they say,

00:04:16.322 --> 00:04:16.623
you know,

00:04:16.623 --> 00:04:17.490
they'll make an argument

00:04:17.490 --> 00:04:19.592
that the case is weak, that it shouldn't

00:04:19.592 --> 00:04:20.326
go forward.

00:04:20.326 --> 00:04:21.127
They have compelling

00:04:21.127 --> 00:04:23.263
evidence of innocence.

00:04:23.263 --> 00:04:24.330
And then they'll usually argue

00:04:24.330 --> 00:04:25.431
some level of

00:04:25.431 --> 00:04:27.033
it would be unfair to go forward

00:04:27.033 --> 00:04:28.868
against against their client

00:04:28.868 --> 00:04:31.070
if justice were to do so.

00:04:31.070 --> 00:04:32.405
You typically don't hear

00:04:32.405 --> 00:04:33.406
anything said

00:04:33.406 --> 00:04:35.141
on the other side of the table.

00:04:35.141 --> 00:04:37.343
DOJ will usually listen

00:04:37.343 --> 00:04:38.911
to the presentation.

00:04:38.911 --> 00:04:40.480
Give the attorneys all the time

00:04:40.480 --> 00:04:42.482
they all the time they've requested

00:04:42.482 --> 00:04:44.150
and at the end of the presentation say,

00:04:44.150 --> 00:04:45.585
thank you very much for coming.

00:04:45.585 --> 00:04:46.552
We'll give you a call

00:04:46.552 --> 00:04:48.721
if we have anything to tell you.

00:04:48.721 --> 00:04:51.257
It's a it's a long shot strategy.

00:04:51.424 --> 00:04:52.458
It's worth the effort

00:04:52.458 --> 00:04:53.793
if you're a defense attorney,

00:04:53.793 --> 00:04:55.161
but it's certainly not worth

00:04:55.161 --> 00:04:56.296
pinning your hopes on.

00:04:56.296 --> 00:04:57.630
So when we hear that

00:04:57.630 --> 00:04:59.032
the former president's attorneys

00:04:59.032 --> 00:05:01.134
are claiming prosecutorial misconduct,

00:05:01.134 --> 00:05:02.101
it may not be that

00:05:02.101 --> 00:05:04.237
they have some smoking gun.

00:05:04.237 --> 00:05:05.672
They have some heretofore

00:05:05.672 --> 00:05:07.940
unheard of evidence.

00:05:07.940 --> 00:05:10.710
This may just be a last ditch effort to

00:05:10.843 --> 00:05:11.811
do whatever they can.

00:05:13.112 --> 00:05:14.280
Yeah, I think it's highly

00:05:14.280 --> 00:05:15.815
unlikely that they have

00:05:15.815 --> 00:05:18.084
significant evidence of misconduct simply

00:05:18.084 --> 00:05:19.452
because so many elements

00:05:19.452 --> 00:05:20.720
of this investigation

00:05:20.720 --> 00:05:22.088
have been ferociously

00:05:22.088 --> 00:05:23.756
litigated between the two sides.

00:05:23.756 --> 00:05:24.824
So you've had a lot

00:05:24.824 --> 00:05:26.993
of judicial intervention

00:05:26.993 --> 00:05:28.695
and oversight already

00:05:28.695 --> 00:05:29.796
in this investigation.

00:05:29.796 --> 00:05:30.830
We had, of course,

00:05:30.830 --> 00:05:33.299
a search warrant that that took place.

00:05:33.666 --> 00:05:36.102
Justice went back in front of

00:05:36.102 --> 00:05:37.770
the judge and requested sanctions

00:05:37.770 --> 00:05:39.439
against the Trump attorneys.

00:05:39.439 --> 00:05:40.473
They've gone in and

00:05:40.473 --> 00:05:42.208
and pursued an effort to pierce

00:05:42.208 --> 00:05:43.576
the attorney client privilege

00:05:43.576 --> 00:05:45.244
to get evidence from Trump's attorney

00:05:45.244 --> 00:05:45.878
Evan Corcoran.

00:05:45.878 --> 00:05:46.913
So there's been a lot

00:05:46.913 --> 00:05:48.715
of judicial intervention would seem that

00:05:48.715 --> 00:05:49.882
if there was actual misconduct,

00:05:49.882 --> 00:05:50.883
that would have come out.

00:05:50.883 --> 00:05:53.319
But my suspicion here, Anderson,

00:05:53.319 --> 00:05:54.354
is that Trump's attorneys

00:05:54.354 --> 00:05:56.356
know the only way

00:05:56.389 --> 00:05:58.157
to get an attorney general

00:05:58.157 --> 00:05:59.192
to really weigh in

00:05:59.192 --> 00:06:00.126
and remove

00:06:00.126 --> 00:06:03.329
or stop a special counsel has to be on

00:06:03.730 --> 00:06:05.431
finding of misconduct

00:06:05.431 --> 00:06:07.200
under the special counsel regulations.

00:06:07.200 --> 00:06:09.001
That's really the only way

00:06:09.001 --> 00:06:10.136
you can turn off

00:06:10.136 --> 00:06:10.803
an attorney general

00:06:10.803 --> 00:06:13.005
can turn off the special counsel effort.

00:06:13.005 --> 00:06:13.806
So if they claim that

00:06:13.806 --> 00:06:15.541
the special counsel engage in misconduct,

00:06:15.541 --> 00:06:16.876
they can essentially fire him,

00:06:16.876 --> 00:06:18.311
but they have to report that to Congress.

00:06:18.311 --> 00:06:20.046
So that is a big ask.

00:06:20.046 --> 00:06:22.048
That's literally swinging for the fences.

00:06:22.048 --> 00:06:24.050
And based on your experience and what you

00:06:24.083 --> 00:06:25.952
what you see publicly

00:06:25.952 --> 00:06:27.787
do you believe Jack Smith is near the end

00:06:27.787 --> 00:06:28.921
of his investigation?

00:06:29.989 --> 00:06:30.623
I do.

00:06:30.623 --> 00:06:32.525
I do. I think it can come very quickly.

00:06:32.525 --> 00:06:33.559
These meetings

00:06:33.559 --> 00:06:34.727
with defense attorneys

00:06:34.727 --> 00:06:36.329
trying to basically plead their case

00:06:36.329 --> 00:06:37.430
before it's indicted

00:06:37.430 --> 00:06:38.765
usually come at the very,

00:06:38.765 --> 00:06:39.332
very end,

00:06:39.332 --> 00:06:41.067
right before the prosecutor

00:06:41.067 --> 00:06:42.301
goes before the grand jury

00:06:42.301 --> 00:06:44.704
and asks them to indict the case.

00:06:44.704 --> 00:06:46.072
So I think we can see that happen

00:06:46.072 --> 00:06:47.473
really any time now

00:06:47.473 --> 00:06:49.742
and certainly within the next few weeks.

00:06:49.742 --> 00:06:50.443
We've also seen

00:06:50.443 --> 00:06:51.811
all the major witnesses

00:06:51.811 --> 00:06:52.545
that we're aware of.

00:06:52.545 --> 00:06:53.012
Most of them

00:06:53.012 --> 00:06:53.679
anyway, we know

00:06:53.679 --> 00:06:54.280
have already been

00:06:54.280 --> 00:06:55.681
in front of the grand jury

00:06:55.681 --> 00:06:57.483
We already understand there's

00:06:57.483 --> 00:06:58.651
there's a lot of evidence

00:06:58.651 --> 00:06:59.585
they have to work with here

00:06:59.585 --> 00:06:59.919
just from

00:06:59.919 --> 00:07:00.253
the things

00:07:00.253 --> 00:07:01.421
that have been publicly reported.

00:07:01.421 --> 00:07:03.122
They like we have much more than that.

00:07:03.122 --> 00:07:05.558
So I think it's reasonable to expect

00:07:05.558 --> 00:07:06.259
that this thing is

00:07:06.259 --> 00:07:07.860
in its in its final stages

00:07:07.860 --> 00:07:09.796
on the investigative side.

00:07:09.796 --> 00:07:09.896
Well,

00:07:09.962 --> 00:07:10.897
what do you make of the Kaitlan Collins

00:07:10.897 --> 00:07:11.864
reporting about the Mar

00:07:11.864 --> 00:07:13.099
a Lago pool flood

00:07:13.099 --> 00:07:15.268
raising suspicions among prosecutors

00:07:17.303 --> 00:07:18.671
Yeah, that's a really weird one.

00:07:18.671 --> 00:07:20.006
I mean, it certainly indicates

00:07:20.006 --> 00:07:20.239
gives you

00:07:20.239 --> 00:07:20.940
an indication

00:07:20.940 --> 00:07:23.676
of the high degree of suspicion that

00:07:24.544 --> 00:07:26.078
each side has of the other.

00:07:26.078 --> 00:07:27.213
And certainly DOJ

00:07:27.213 --> 00:07:28.948
is investigating obstruction here.

00:07:28.948 --> 00:07:30.082
We know that from the search

00:07:30.082 --> 00:07:31.250
warrant application.

00:07:31.250 --> 00:07:31.651
So they're going

00:07:31.651 --> 00:07:33.786
to look at every possible act

00:07:33.786 --> 00:07:34.754
as a possible,

00:07:34.754 --> 00:07:35.888
you know, every every act

00:07:35.888 --> 00:07:38.157
as a possible element of obstruction

00:07:38.157 --> 00:07:39.592
in this case.

00:07:39.592 --> 00:07:41.761
With with the witnesses already

00:07:41.761 --> 00:07:43.262
saying that the flood didn't

00:07:43.262 --> 00:07:43.996
really damage

00:07:43.996 --> 00:07:45.331
the computers

00:07:45.331 --> 00:07:46.999
it's hard to say

00:07:46.999 --> 00:07:47.800
until we hear that

00:07:47.800 --> 00:07:50.002
the Trump team is actually claiming

00:07:50.169 --> 00:07:52.205
that they cannot produce

00:07:52.205 --> 00:07:54.173
the videos requested under the search,

00:07:54.173 --> 00:07:55.575
under the subpoenas

00:07:55.575 --> 00:07:58.578
because the material was damaged

00:07:58.578 --> 00:07:59.512
by water.

00:07:59.512 --> 00:08:00.279
It's hard to say

00:08:00.279 --> 00:08:01.581
that they're actually using that

00:08:01.581 --> 00:08:03.282
as some sort of a.

00:08:03.282 --> 00:08:05.184
What does obstruction look like? I mean,

00:08:06.452 --> 00:08:08.754
from a legal standpoint.

00:08:08.888 --> 00:08:10.056
So obstruction,

00:08:10.056 --> 00:08:10.423
you know,

00:08:10.423 --> 00:08:11.390
in order to charge

00:08:11.390 --> 00:08:12.358
someone with obstruction,

00:08:12.358 --> 00:08:13.526
you have to be able to prove

00:08:13.526 --> 00:08:14.894
that they intended

00:08:14.894 --> 00:08:17.029
to obstruct an official proceeding.

00:08:17.096 --> 00:08:17.296
Right.

00:08:17.296 --> 00:08:18.431
So an accident,

00:08:18.431 --> 00:08:20.333
the accidental flooding of a room

00:08:20.333 --> 00:08:21.968
with computers in it

00:08:21.968 --> 00:08:24.437
without more evidence of actual intent

00:08:24.570 --> 00:08:26.873
to stop or obstruct the proceeding.

00:08:26.873 --> 00:08:29.075
In this case, the investigation wouldn't

00:08:29.842 --> 00:08:31.277
probably rise to the level

00:08:31.277 --> 00:08:33.312
of a chargeable offense.

00:08:33.312 --> 00:08:35.715
However, conversations with your lawyer

00:08:35.715 --> 00:08:37.683
in which you're lying to your lawyer

00:08:37.683 --> 00:08:39.051
about where the docks are

00:08:39.051 --> 00:08:40.853
and what's available to be searched,

00:08:40.853 --> 00:08:42.121
as is also

00:08:42.121 --> 00:08:43.890
allegedly taking place in this case,

00:08:43.890 --> 00:08:46.993
that could very well be

00:08:46.993 --> 00:08:48.694
end up as a chargeable offense.

00:08:48.694 --> 00:08:49.028
All right.

00:08:49.028 --> 00:08:50.530
Andrew McCabe, appreciate it. Thank you.

